      ut":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1
      seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"FILE README.md\n 1\t# MicroSandbox
      Workspaces (`msw`) 3.1.0\n 2\t\n 3\tA ready-to-run development setup for an Apple Silicon Mac
      with three isolated, persistent Linux microVM workspaces:\n 4\t\n 5\t| Workspace | Purpose |
      Normal live limit | Resize ceiling | Browser name |\n 6\t|---|---|---:|---:|---|\n 7\t| `dev`
      | Main/work development | 8 CPU, 32 GB RAM | 48 GB RAM | `dev.msw.test` |\n 8\t| `playgrounds`
      | Experiments | 4 CPU, 32 GB RAM | 48 GB RAM | `playgrounds.msw.test` |\n 9\t| `personal` |
      Personal projects | 6 CPU, 16 GB RAM | 32 GB RAM | `personal.msw.test` |\n 10\t\n 11\tEach
      workspace has its own Ubuntu system, repositories, Docker daemon, images, volumes,
      credentials, processes, and public-internet connection. Code and Docker data live on
      independent persistent ext4 volumes. Zed and Ghostty remain native macOS applications and
      connect over SSH.\n 12\t\n 13\t## Install\n 14\t\n 15\t```bash\n 16\tunzip
      microsandbox-workspaces-v3.1.0.zip\n 17\tcd microsandbox-workspaces\n 18\t./setup.sh\n
      19\texec zsh -l\n 20\t```\n 21\t\n 22\t`setup.sh` installs the host tools, builds the common
      development image, creates all three workspaces, publishes the configured localhost ports,
      configures SSH/Zed integration, and finishes with a live deep check.\n 23\t\n 24\tThen set
      your commit identity:\n 25\t\n 26\t```bash\n 27\tmsw identity \"Your Name\" you@example.com\n
      28\t```\n 29\t\n 30\tConfigure GitHub separately for each workspace:\n 31\t\n 32\t```bash\n
      33\tmsw github setup dev OWNER/VERIFICATION-REPO\n 34\tmsw github setup playgrounds
      OWNER/VERIFICATION-REPO\n 35\tmsw github setup personal OWNER/VERIFICATION-REPO\n 36\t```\n
      37\t\n 38\tEach command asks for two fine-grained GitHub tokens and verifies the complete
      permission boundary automatically: read/clone from the VM, rejected guest push, successful
      host-only push, and cleanup. See [`docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md`](docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md).\n 39\t\n
      40\t## Daily use\n 41\t\n 42\t```bash\n 43\t# Enter /workspace in Ghostty\n 44\tmsw dev\n
      45\tmsw playgrounds\n 46\tmsw personal\n 47\t\n 48\t# Enter a nested repository\n 49\tmsw dev
      clients/acme/backend\n 50\t\n 51\t# Clone into a nested folder\n 52\tmsw clone dev OWNER/REPO
      clients/acme/backend\n 53\t\n 54\t# Open in Zed\n 55\tmsw zed dev clients/acme/backend\n
      56\t\n 57\t# Open a running website in your Mac browser\n 58\tmsw open dev 3000\n 59\tmsw open
      playgrounds 5173\n 60\t\n 61\t# Explicitly push the current committed branch from the Mac\n
      62\tmsw push dev clients/acme/backend\n 63\t\n 64\t# Back up every VM and persistent volume\n
      65\tmsw backup\n 66\t```\n 67\t\n 68\tA service must listen on `0.0.0.0` inside the VM or
      container. The common development ports are already published to each workspace's dedicated
      loopback IP, so all three can use port 3000 simultaneously.\n 69\t\n 70\t## Documentation\n
      71\t\n 72\t- [Complete setup guide](docs/SETUP-GUIDE.md)\n 73\t- [GitHub permissions and push
      guide](docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md)\n 74\t- [Command cheatsheet](docs/MSW-CHEATSHEET.md)\n 75\t-
      [Test report](docs/TEST-REPORT.md)\n 76\t\n 77\tInstalled documentation is also available from
      any terminal:\n 78\t\n 79\t```bash\n 80\tmsw docs setup\n 81\tmsw docs github\n 82\tmsw docs
      cheatsheet\n 83\tmsw docs tests\n 84\t```\n 85\t\n 86\t## Trust boundary\n 87\t\n 88\tEvery
      process and agent inside one workspace can access everything in that workspace. The three
      workspaces are separate from one another and no Mac folder, Mac Docker socket, or Mac SSH
      agent is mounted into them. The guest GitHub credential is read-only and host-held; the write
      credential remains in macOS Keychain and is used only by the explicit `msw push` path.\n
      89\t\n 90\tFull public internet access means an untrusted agent can still transmit files it
      can read to an unrelated internet service. This setup prevents direct access to your Mac and
      prevents GitHub pushes with the guest credential; it is not a data-loss-prevention
      system.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"FILE docs/MSW-CHEATSHEET.md\n 1\t# MSW command
      cheatsheet\n 2\t\n 3\t## Enter and edit\n 4\t\n 5\t```bash\n 6\tmsw dev [PATH] # Ghostty shell
      in dev\n 7\tmsw playgrounds [PATH] # Shell in playgrounds\n 8\tmsw personal [PATH] # Shell in
      personal\n 9\tmsw shell WORKSPACE [PATH] # Generic form\n 10\tmsw zed WORKSPACE [PATH] # Open
      in Zed\n 11\tmsw exec WORKSPACE COMMAND... # Run command from /workspace\n 12\t```\n 13\t\n
      14\tExamples:\n 15\t\n 16\t```bash\n 17\tmsw dev\n 18\tmsw dev clients/acme/backend\n 19\tmsw
      zed personal apps/site\n 20\tmsw exec playgrounds node --version\n 21\t```\n 22\t\n 23\t##
      Repositories\n 24\t\n 25\t```bash\n 26\tmsw clone WORKSPACE OWNER/REPO [PATH]\n 27\tmsw repos
      WORKSPACE\n 28\tmsw pull WORKSPACE [PATH|all]\n 29\tmsw identity \"NAME\" EMAIL
      [WORKSPACE|all]\n 30\t```\n 31\t\n 32\tExamples:\n 33\t\n 34\t```bash\n 35\tmsw clone dev
      acme/backend clients/acme/backend\n 36\tmsw clone dev acme/frontend clients/acme/frontend\n
      37\tmsw repos dev\n 38\tmsw pull dev clients/acme/backend\n 39\tmsw pull dev all\n 40\tmsw
      identity \"Ada Lovelace\" ada@example.com\n 41\t```\n 42\t\n 43\t## GitHub permissions\n
      44\t\n 45\t```bash\n 46\tmsw github setup WORKSPACE OWNER/VERIFICATION-REPO\n 47\tmsw github
      verify WORKSPACE [OWNER/REPO]\n 48\tmsw github status [WORKSPACE|all]\n 49\tmsw github remove
      WORKSPACE\n 50\t```\n 51\t\n 52\tSet up every group:\n 53\t\n 54\t```bash\n 55\tmsw github
      setup dev OWNER/msw-verification\n 56\tmsw github setup playgrounds OWNER/msw-verification\n
      57\tmsw github setup personal OWNER/msw-verification\n 58\t```\n 59\t\n 60\t## Push from the
      Mac\n 61\t\n 62\t```bash\n 63\tmsw push WORKSPACE PATH\n 64\tmsw push WORKSPACE PATH --yes\n
      65\tmsw push WORKSPACE PATH --force-with-lease\n 66\t```\n 67\t\n 68\tExamples:\n 69\t\n
      70\t```bash\n 71\tmsw push dev clients/acme/backend\n 72\tmsw push personal apps/site --yes\n
      73\tmsw push dev clients/acme/backend --force-with-lease\n 74\t```\n 75\t\n 76\tDefault pushes
      are fast-forward-only. Only the current committed branch is sent.\n 77\t\n 78\t## Websites and
      ports\n 79\t\n 80\t```bash\n 81\tmsw open WORKSPACE [PORT] [http|https]\n 82\tmsw url
      WORKSPACE [PORT] [http|https]\n 83\tmsw ports [WORKSPACE|all]\n 84\tmsw tunnel WORKSPACE
      REMOTE_PORT [LOCAL_PORT]\n 85\t```\n 86\t\n 87\tExamples:\n 88\t\n 89\t```bash\n 90\tmsw open
      dev 3000\n 91\tmsw open playgrounds 5173\n 92\tmsw url personal 8080\n 93\tmsw tunnel dev
      12345\n 94\tmsw tunnel dev 12345 9001\n 95\t```\n 96\t\n 97\tFixed names:\n 98\t\n
      99\t```text\n 100\thttp://dev.msw.test:<port>\n 101\thttp://playgrounds.msw.test:<port>\n
      102\thttp://personal.msw.test:<port>\n 103\t```\n 104\t\n 105\tThe service inside the
      VM/container must listen on `0.0.0.0`.\n 106\t\n 107\tPrepublished TCP ports:\n 108\t\n
      109\t```text\n 110\t1234, 1337, 24678-24679, 3000-3010, 3100, 3333,\n 111\t3306-3308,
      4000-4005, 4173, 4200, 4321, 5001-5005,\n 112\t5173-5180, 5432-5435, 5555, 6006, 6379-6382,\n
      113\t7001-7005, 8000-8010, 8080-8090, 8787, 8888,\n 114\t9000-9005, 9229-9230, 27017-27019\n
      115\t```\n 116\t\n 117\tPorts 24678 and 24679 are reserved for deep health checks.\n 118\t\n
      119\t## Docker\n 120\t\n 121\tInside a workspace:\n 122\t\n 123\t```bash\n 124\tdocker compose
      up --build\n 125\tdocker compose up -d --build\n 126\tdocker compose ps\n 127\tdocker compose
      logs -f\n 128\tdocker compose down\n 129\t```\n 130\t\n 131\tFrom the Mac:\n 132\t\n
      133\t```bash\n 134\tmsw exec dev docker ps\n 135\tmsw exec dev docker compose -f
      /workspace/PROJECT/compose.yml ps\n 136\t```\n 137\t\n 138\tCleanup:\n 139\t\n 140\t```bash\n
      141\tmsw clean [WORKSPACE|all] # Preserve Docker volumes\n 142\tmsw clean [WORKSPACE|all]
      --volumes # Also delete unused volumes\n 143\t```\n 144\t\n 145\t## Lifecycle\n 146\t\n
      147\t```bash\n 148\tmsw start [WORKSPACE|all]\n 149\tmsw stop [WORKSPACE|all]\n 150\tmsw
      restart [WORKSPACE|all]\n 151\tmsw status\n 152\tmsw logs WORKSPACE [MSB LOG OPTIONS]\n
      153\t```\n 154\t\n 155\tExamples:\n 156\t\n 157\t```bash\n 158\tmsw start all\n 159\tmsw stop
      playgrounds\n 160\tmsw restart dev\n 161\tmsw status\n 162\tmsw logs dev\n 163\t```\n 164\t\n
      165\tEntering with Ghostty, opening in Zed, or using `msw open` starts a stopped VM
      automatically.\n 166\t\n 167\t## Resources and maintenance\n 168\t\n 169\t```bash\n 170\tmsw
      metrics [WORKSPACE|all]\n 171\tmsw disk [WORKSPACE|all]\n 172\tmsw resize WORKSPACE MEMORY
      [CPUS]\n 173\tmsw upgrade [WORKSPACE|all]\n 174\tmsw update\n 175\tmsw check\n 176\tmsw check
      --deep\n 177\tmsw host repair\n 178\t```\n 179\t\n 180\tExamples:\n 181\t\n 182\t```bash\n
      183\tmsw metrics dev\n 184\tmsw disk all\n 185\tmsw resize dev 32G 10\n 186\tmsw upgrade all\n
      187\tmsw check --deep\n 188\t```\n 189\t\n 190\t## Backup and restore\n 191\t\n 192\t```bash\n
      193\tmsw backup [DIRECTORY]\n 194\tmsw restore ARCHIVE\n 195\tmsw restore ARCHIVE --yes\n
      196\t```\n 197\t\n 198\tExamples:\n 199\t\n 200\t```bash\n 201\tmsw backup\n 202\tmsw backup
      /Volumes/EncryptedBackup/MicroSandbox\n 203\tmsw restore
      ~/Backups/microsandbox/microsandbox-all-20260805-120000.tar.zst\n 204\t```\n 205\t\n 206\tA
      backup automatically stops and restarts only the VMs that were running. Restore leaves all VMs
      stopped.\n 207\t\n 208\t## Documentation and version\n 209\t\n 210\t```bash\n 211\tmsw docs\n
      212\tmsw docs cheatsheet\n 213\tmsw docs setup\n 214\tmsw docs github\n 215\tmsw docs tests\n
      216\tmsw version\n 217\tmsw help\n 218\t```\n 219\t\n 220\t## Installer repair modes\n 221\t\n
      222\tRun from the extracted package directory:\n 223\t\n 224\t```bash\n 225\t./setup.sh\n
      226\t./setup.sh --rebuild-base\n 227\t./setup.sh --recreate-workspaces\n 228\t./setup.sh
      --reset-config\n 229\t```\n 230\t\n 231\tPersistent `/workspace` and Docker runtime volumes
      survive workspace-root recreation. Always keep current backups before destructive
      maintenance.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"FILE docs/SETUP-GUIDE.md\n 1\t# MSW setup
      guide\n 2\t\n 3\tThis guide installs three persistent MicroSandbox development workspaces on
      an Apple Silicon Mac:\n 4\t\n 5\t- `dev`\n 6\t- `playgrounds`\n 7\t- `personal`\n 8\t\n 9\tThe
      packaged defaults allow up to 80 GB of live VM memory across the three workspaces, with resize
      ceilings up to 128 GB. Ensure the Mac has sufficient RAM. All commands below run in Ghostty
      unless stated otherwise.\n 10\t\n 11\t## 1. Install\n 12\t\n 13\tUnzip the package and run the
      installer:\n 14\t\n 15\t```bash\n 16\tcd ~/Downloads\n 17\tunzip
      microsandbox-workspaces-v3.1.0.zip\n 18\tcd microsandbox-workspaces\n 19\t./setup.sh\n
      20\t```\n 21\t\n 22\tThe installer may request your macOS administrator password to add three
      private loopback addresses and local browser names. It then:\n 23\t\n 24\t1. Installs Homebrew
      when missing.\n 25\t2. Installs GNU tar, zstd, Git LFS, and MicroSandbox.\n 26\t3. Runs
      MicroSandbox diagnostics.\n 27\t4. Builds one reusable Ubuntu 24.04 ARM64 development
      snapshot.\n 28\t5. Creates `dev`, `playgrounds`, and `personal` from that snapshot.\n 29\t6.
      Attaches a separate persistent ext4 workspace volume and Docker-runtime volume to each VM.\n
      30\t7. Configures Docker Engine, Compose, Buildx, Git, GitHub CLI, mise, Node LTS, pnpm, uv,
      Python, zsh, and common development tools.\n 31\t8. Configures fixed local browser names and
      SSH aliases.\n 32\t9. Runs `msw check --deep` against all three VMs.\n 33\t\n 34\tWhen it
      finishes:\n 35\t\n 36\t```bash\n 37\texec zsh -l\n 38\t```\n 39\t\n 40\tThe installation is
      complete only when the installer prints:\n 41\t\n 42\t```text\n 43\tall live VM, Docker, SSH,
      internet, and published-port checks passed\n 44\t```\n 45\t\n 46\t## 2. Set your Git
      identity\n 47\t\n 48\tApply one identity to all three workspaces:\n 49\t\n 50\t```bash\n
      51\tmsw identity \"Your Full Name\" you@example.com\n 52\t```\n 53\t\n 54\tOr configure one
      workspace only:\n 55\t\n 56\t```bash\n 57\tmsw identity \"Your Full Name\" work@example.com
      dev\n 58\t```\n 59\t\n 60\t## 3. Configure GitHub\n 61\t\n 62\tFollow
      [GITHUB-SETUP.md](GITHUB-SETUP.md). The final commands are:\n 63\t\n 64\t```bash\n 65\tmsw
      github setup dev OWNER/VERIFICATION-REPO\n 66\tmsw github setup playgrounds
      OWNER/VERIFICATION-REPO\n 67\tmsw github setup personal OWNER/VERIFICATION-REPO\n 68\t```\n
      69\t\n 70\tYou may use the same verification repository for several workspaces when both token
      pairs include it. Each command performs its own end-to-end permission check.\n 71\t\n 72\t##
      4. Enter a workspace from Ghostty\n 73\t\n 74\t```bash\n 75\tmsw dev\n 76\tmsw playgrounds\n
      77\tmsw personal\n 78\t```\n 79\t\n 80\tEach command starts a stopped VM automatically and
      opens a zsh login shell in `/workspace`.\n 81\t\n 82\tEnter a nested project directly:\n
      83\t\n 84\t```bash\n 85\tmsw dev clients/acme/backend\n 86\tmsw personal apps/my-site\n
      87\t```\n 88\t\n 89\t## 5. Clone repositories\n 90\t\n 91\tClone at the workspace root:\n
      92\t\n 93\t```bash\n 94\tmsw clone dev OWNER/backend\n 95\t```\n 96\t\n 97\tClone into any
      nested folder; missing parent folders are created:\n 98\t\n 99\t```bash\n 100\tmsw clone dev
      OWNER/backend clients/acme/backend\n 101\tmsw clone dev OWNER/frontend clients/acme/frontend\n
      102\tmsw clone personal OWNER/site apps/site\n 103\t```\n 104\t\n 105\tList every repository
      in a workspace:\n 106\t\n 107\t```bash\n 108\tmsw repos dev\n 109\t```\n 110\t\n 111\tYou can
      also clone normally from inside a workspace:\n 112\t\n 113\t```bash\n 114\tmsw dev\n
      115\tmkdir -p clients/acme\n 116\tcd clients/acme\n 117\tgit clone
      https://github.com/OWNER/backend.git\n 118\t```\n 119\t\n 120\tDifferent repositories never
      require Git worktrees. A worktree is useful only when two actors need separate checkouts of
      different branches of the same repository at the same time.\n 121\t\n 122\t## 6. Open projects
      in Zed\n 123\t\n 124\tIn Zed, run `cli: install cli binary` once from the command palette.\n
      125\t\n 126\tThen open a project from Ghostty:\n 127\t\n 128\t```bash\n 129\tmsw zed dev
      clients/acme/backend\n 130\tmsw zed personal apps/site\n 131\t```\n 132\t\n 133\tOpen the
      whole workspace when useful:\n 134\t\n 135\t```bash\n 136\tmsw zed playgrounds\n 137\t```\n
      138\t\n 139\tZed's interface stays on macOS. Files, terminals, language servers, tasks,
      builds, tests, and remote agent processes run inside the selected VM.\n 140\t\n 141\t## 7. Run
      websites and Docker projects\n 142\t\n 143\tRun services normally inside the VM:\n 144\t\n
      145\t```bash\n 146\tmsw dev clients/acme/backend\n 147\tdocker compose up --build\n 148\t```\n
      149\t\n 150\tFor background services:\n 151\t\n 152\t```bash\n 153\tdocker compose up -d
      --build\n 154\t```\n 155\t\n 156\tOpen a published port from macOS:\n 157\t\n 158\t```bash\n
      159\tmsw open dev 3000\n 160\tmsw open dev 5173\n 161\tmsw open personal 8080\n 162\t```\n
      163\t\n 164\tEquivalent URLs include:\n 165\t\n 166\t```text\n 167\thttp://dev.msw.test:3000\n
      168\thttp://playgrounds.msw.test:3000\n 169\thttp://personal.msw.test:3000\n 170\t```\n
      171\t\n 172\tThe same port can be active in all three VMs because each workspace has its own
      loopback IP.\n 173\t\n 174\tThe server inside the VM must bind to `0.0.0.0`, not only
      `127.0.0.1`. Examples:\n 175\t\n 176\t```bash\n 177\t# Vite\n 178\tnpm run dev -- --host
      0.0.0.0\n 179\t\n 180\t# Next.js\n 181\tnpm run dev -- --hostname 0.0.0.0\n 182\t\n 183\t#
      Uvicorn\n 184\tuv run uvicorn app:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000\n 185\t```\n 186\t\n 187\tA
      Docker Compose service typically needs both an internal all-interface bind and a published
      port:\n 188\t\n 189\t```yaml\n 190\tservices:\n 191\t app:\n 192\t build: .\n 193\t ports:\n
      194\t - \"3000:3000\"\n 195\t volumes:\n 196\t - .:/app\n 197\t```\n 198\t\n 199\tFor an
      uncommon unlisted port:\n 200\t\n 201\t```bash\n 202\tmsw tunnel dev 12345\n 203\t```\n
      204\t\n 205\tThen open `http://localhost:12345`. Keep that tunnel command running until
      finished.\n 206\t\n 207\t## 8. Pull, commit, and push\n 208\t\n 209\tInside a workspace,
      normal local Git operations work:\n 210\t\n 211\t```bash\n 212\tgit switch -c
      feature/example\n 213\t# edit files\n 214\tgit add .\n 215\tgit commit -m \"Implement
      example\"\n 216\tgit pull --ff-only\n 217\t```\n 218\t\n 219\tThe VM can fetch and pull
      private repositories selected in its read token. Direct guest pushes are rejected by GitHub.\n
      220\t\n 221\tPush explicitly from a Mac terminal:\n 222\t\n 223\t```bash\n 224\tmsw push dev
      clients/acme/backend\n 225\t```\n 226\t\n 227\tReview the displayed repository, branch,
      commit, and commits, then type `PUSH`.\n 228\t\n 229\tFor an automation-friendly
      confirmation:\n 230\t\n 231\t```bash\n 232\tmsw push dev clients/acme/backend --yes\n
      233\t```\n 234\t\n 235\tA non-fast-forward push is refused. After deliberate review, use an
      exact lease-protected force push:\n 236\t\n 237\t```bash\n 238\tmsw push dev
      clients/acme/backend --force-with-lease\n 239\t```\n 240\t\n 241\tOnly the current committed
      branch is transferred. Dirty files, other local branches, and tags are not pushed.\n 242\t\n
      243\t## 9. Lifecycle and resource use\n 244\t\n 245\t```bash\n 246\tmsw status\n 247\tmsw
      start dev\n 248\tmsw stop dev\n 249\tmsw restart personal\n 250\tmsw stop all\n 251\t```\n
      252\t\n 253\tAll three can run together with the defaults. Monitor them with:\n 254\t\n
      255\t```bash\n 256\tmsw metrics dev\n 257\tmsw disk all\n 258\t```\n 259\t\n 260\tResize
      within the configured ceiling:\n 261\t\n 262\t```bash\n 263\tmsw resize dev 32G 10\n
      264\t```\n 265\t\n 266\t## 10. Back up everything\n 267\t\n 268\tCreate a cold compressed
      backup:\n 269\t\n 270\t```bash\n 271\tmsw backup\n 272\t```\n 273\t\n 274\tDefault
      destination:\n 275\t\n 276\t```text\n 277\t~/Backups/microsandbox/\n 278\t```\n 279\t\n
      280\tUse encrypted external storage for a disaster-recovery copy:\n 281\t\n 282\t```bash\n
      283\tmsw backup /Volumes/EncryptedBackup/MicroSandbox\n 284\t```\n 285\t\n 286\tThe command
      records which workspaces are running, flushes and stops them, archives all VM roots and
      persistent volumes with sparse-file support, writes a SHA-256 checksum and info file, and
      restarts only the workspaces that were previously running.\n 287\t\n 288\tThe archive includes
      code, Docker images, Docker volumes, databases, guest credentials, and VM state. macOS
      Keychain tokens are deliberately excluded. Treat the archive as sensitive anyway.\n 289\t\n
      290\tRestore:\n 291\t\n 292\t```bash\n 293\tmsw restore
      ~/Backups/microsandbox/microsandbox-all-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.tar.zst\n 294\t```\n 295\t\n 296\tType
      `RESTORE` after reviewing the warning. The restore validates the checksum and archive paths,
      installs into a transaction, checks the restored MicroSandbox state, and rolls back
      automatically if health checks fail. Workspaces remain stopped afterward:\n 297\t\n
      298\t```bash\n 299\tmsw host repair\n 300\tmsw check --deep\n 301\tmsw start all\n 302\t```\n
      303\t\n 304\tFor best compatibility, restore with the same MSW/MicroSandbox generation used to
      make the backup.\n 305\t\n 306\t## 11. Updates and maintenance\n 307\t\n 308\t```bash\n 309\t#
      Update MicroSandbox and run diagnostics\n 310\tmsw update\n 311\t\n 312\t# Upgrade Ubuntu
      packages\n 313\tmsw upgrade all\n 314\t\n 315\t# Prune unused Docker objects, preserving
      volumes\n 316\tmsw clean all\n 317\t\n 318\t# Also delete unused Docker volumes—destructive
      for unused database volumes\n 319\tmsw clean all --volumes\n 320\t\n 321\t# Full live
      verification\n 322\tmsw check --deep\n 323\t```\n 324\t\n 325\tRe-run the packaged installer
      safely at any time:\n 326\t\n 327\t```bash\n 328\t./setup.sh\n 329\t```\n 330\t\n 331\tUseful
      repair/rebuild modes:\n 332\t\n 333\t```bash\n 334\t# Rebuild the reusable tool snapshot,
      preserving existing VM data\n 335\t./setup.sh --rebuild-base\n 336\t\n 337\t# Recreate VM
      roots while preserving workspace and Docker volumes\n 338\t./setup.sh --recreate-workspaces\n
      339\t\n 340\t# Restore packaged config and recreate roots; persistent data volumes survive\n
      341\t./setup.sh --reset-config\n 342\t```\n 343\t\n 344\t## 12. Important boundaries\n 345\t\n
      346\t- `dev`, `playgrounds`, and `personal` are separate trust domains.\n 347\t- Within one
      workspace, agents can access all repositories, processes, guest credentials, and Docker
      resources in that workspace.\n 348\t- No host directory, host Docker socket, or host SSH agent
      is shared by this setup.\n 349\t- The public network profile permits internet access but
      blocks direct host/private-network access.\n 350\t- A guest read token cannot push to GitHub,
      but unrestricted internet still permits source-code exfiltration to unrelated services.\n
      351\t- Prefer ARM64 or multi-architecture container images for native M4
      performance.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"FILE docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md\n 1\t# GitHub setup:
      read-only inside VMs, explicit pushes from the Mac\n 2\t\n 3\tThis setup uses two different
      fine-grained personal access tokens for each workspace:\n 4\t\n 5\t1. A **guest read token**.
      It lets the VM clone, fetch, and pull selected private repositories. GitHub rejects pushes
      made with it.\n 6\t2. A **host write token**. It stays in macOS Keychain and is used only when
      you explicitly run `msw push` or the permission verifier.\n 7\t\n 8\tAgents can create
      branches, edit, commit, merge, rebase, and inspect history locally without any write
      credential.\n 9\t\n 10\tFor a workspace that only needs private read access, omit the host
      credential:\n 11\t\n 12\t```bash\n 13\tmsw github setup playgrounds OWNER/msw-verification
      --read-only\n 14\t```\n 15\t\n 16\tThe command stores only the guest read token and removes
      any host write token for that workspace.\n 17\t\n 18\t## Before creating tokens\n 19\t\n
      20\tChoose one repository that will be used to verify permissions. It must:\n 21\t\n 22\t-
      Belong to the same resource owner as the other selected repositories.\n 23\t- Be selected in
      both tokens.\n 24\t- Permit the host token to create and delete a temporary branch.\n 25\t- Be
      safe for a temporary empty verification commit and branch; the branch is deleted
      automatically.\n 26\t\n 27\tA small private repository such as `OWNER/msw-verification` is
      ideal.\n 28\t\n 29\tFine-grained tokens are scoped to one resource owner. A workspace that
      needs private repositories from two different owners normally needs to be split by owner, or
      one set of repositories must use another authentication mechanism. Public repositories do not
      require the private read token.\n 30\t\n 31\t## Token 1: guest read-only token\n 32\t\n 33\tIn
      GitHub, create a new **fine-grained personal access token** with:\n 34\t\n 35\t```text\n
      36\tToken name: msw-dev-read (or msw-personal-read, etc.)\n 37\tExpiration: your preferred
      rotation period\n 38\tResource owner: the user or organization owning the repositories\n
      39\tRepository access: Only select repositories\n 40\tSelected repos: every repository this VM
      may read, including verification repo\n 41\t```\n 42\t\n 43\tRepository permissions:\n 44\t\n
      45\t```text\n 46\tContents: Read-only\n 47\tMetadata: Read-only (GitHub adds this
      automatically)\n 48\t```\n 49\t\n 50\tLeave every other permission at **No access** unless a
      tool genuinely needs read access. Optional examples are Issues: Read-only or Pull requests:
      Read-only. Do not grant any write permission.\n 51\t\n 52\tAn organization may require an
      administrator to approve the token before private-repository access works.\n 53\t\n 54\t##
      Token 2: host write token\n 55\t\n 56\tCreate a second fine-grained token:\n 57\t\n
      58\t```text\n 59\tToken name: msw-dev-host-write\n 60\tExpiration: your preferred rotation
      period\n 61\tResource owner: same owner as the read token\n 62\tRepository access: Only select
      repositories\n 63\tSelected repos: only repositories you are willing to push from this
      workspace,\n 64\t including the verification repo\n 65\t```\n 66\t\n 67\tRepository
      permissions:\n 68\t\n 69\t```text\n 70\tContents: Read and write\n 71\tMetadata: Read-only
      (automatic)\n 72\t```\n 73\t\n 74\tOnly when you need to push changes under
      `.github/workflows/`, also grant:\n 75\t\n 76\t```text\n 77\tWorkflows: Read and write\n
      78\t```\n 79\t\n 80\tNo administration, secrets, webhooks, organization, or other write
      permissions are required by MSW.\n 81\t\n 82\tThe host token's selected repository list is the
      definitive set of repositories `msw push` can modify.\n 83\t\n 84\t## Configure a workspace\n
      85\t\n 86\tRun:\n 87\t\n 88\t```bash\n 89\tmsw github setup dev OWNER/msw-verification\n
      90\t```\n 91\t\n 92\tPaste the guest read token, then the host write token. Input is hidden.\n
      93\t\n 94\tFor a read-only workspace, add `--read-only`:\n 95\t\n 96\t```bash\n 97\tmsw github
      setup playgrounds OWNER/msw-verification --read-only\n 98\t```\n 99\t\n 100\tThis prompts for
      only the guest token. `msw github verify playgrounds` reruns the read-only check.\n 101\t\n
      102\tThe command performs all of these checks automatically:\n 103\t\n 104\t1. Stores both
      tokens in macOS Keychain under separate services.\n 105\t2. Binds only the read token to the
      VM through MicroSandbox secret substitution.\n 106\t3. Clones the verification repository from
      inside the VM.\n 107\t4. Creates a temporary local branch and commit.\n 108\t5. Confirms that
      a direct guest push is rejected by GitHub.\n 109\t6. Transfers the committed branch through
      the host-only push path.\n 110\t7. Confirms the branch exists remotely.\n 111\t8. Deletes the
      temporary branch with the host-only credential.\n 112\t9. Confirms cleanup.\n 113\t\n 114\tIf
      any step fails, the newly entered credentials are removed and the previously working
      credential pair is restored.\n 115\t\n 116\tRepeat for all groups:\n 117\t\n 118\t```bash\n
      119\tmsw github setup dev OWNER/msw-verification\n 120\tmsw github setup playgrounds
      OWNER/msw-verification\n 121\tmsw github setup personal OWNER/msw-verification\n 122\t```\n
      123\t\n 124\tUsing different selected-repository lists for each pair provides meaningful
      separation. You may use tokens from the same GitHub account.\n 125\t\n 126\tA workspace that
      does not use GitHub needs no `msw github setup` command.\n 127\t\n 128\t## Check or rotate
      credentials\n 129\t\n 130\tShow only credential presence and the recorded verification
      repository; tokens are never printed:\n 131\t\n 132\t```bash\n 133\tmsw github status all\n
      134\t```\n 135\t\n 136\tRerun the permission test:\n 137\t\n 138\t```bash\n 139\tmsw github
      verify dev\n 140\t```\n 141\t\n 142\tRotate either token by running setup again:\n 143\t\n
      144\t```bash\n 145\tmsw github setup dev OWNER/msw-verification\n 146\t```\n 147\t\n
      148\tRemove both credentials and the guest secret binding:\n 149\t\n 150\t```bash\n 151\tmsw
      github remove dev\n 152\t```\n 153\t\n 154\tSetup, verification, and removal are serialized
      per workspace. Setup's verifier retains the inherited lock if its parent exits unexpectedly.
      The lock file may remain after a command, but its kernel-held `lockf` ownership is released
      automatically on exit or crash, so stale files do not block later operations.\n 155\t\n
      156\t## Clone and pull from the VM\n 157\t\n 158\t```bash\n 159\tmsw clone dev OWNER/backend
      clients/acme/backend\n 160\tmsw pull dev clients/acme/backend\n 161\tmsw pull dev all\n
      162\t```\n 163\t\n 164\tInside the VM, ordinary commands work too:\n 165\t\n 166\t```bash\n
      167\tmsw dev clients/acme/backend\n 168\tgit fetch --prune\n 169\tgit pull --ff-only\n
      170\t```\n 171\t\n 172\tThe guest sees a MicroSandbox placeholder, not the real token. The
      real read token remains host-held and is substituted only for requests to `github.com` and
      `api.github.com`.\n 173\t\n 174\t## Commit locally\n 175\t\n 176\tNo GitHub credential is
      needed to commit:\n 177\t\n 178\t```bash\n 179\tgit switch -c feature/new-api\n 180\tgit add
      .\n 181\tgit commit -m \"Add the API\"\n 182\t```\n 183\t\n 184\tA direct push from the VM is
      expected to fail:\n 185\t\n 186\t```bash\n 187\tgit push\n 188\t```\n 189\t\n 190\tThis is
      enforced by the token's server-side GitHub permissions, not merely by a local hook or Git
      setting.\n 191\t\n 192\t## Push from the Mac\n 193\t\n 194\tFrom any macOS terminal:\n 195\t\n
      196\t```bash\n 197\tmsw push dev clients/acme/backend\n 198\t```\n 199\t\n 200\tMSW
      displays:\n 201\t\n 202\t- Workspace\n 203\t- Canonical GitHub repository\n 204\t-
      Workspace-relative path\n 205\t- Current branch\n 206\t- Exact commit\n 207\t- Fast-forward
      status\n 208\t- Commits to be sent\n 209\t\n 210\tType `PUSH` to approve.\n 211\t\n 212\tSkip
      the prompt only when intended:\n 213\t\n 214\t```bash\n 215\tmsw push dev clients/acme/backend
      --yes\n 216\t```\n 217\t\n 218\tThe default push is fast-forward-only. For a deliberate
      history rewrite:\n 219\t\n 220\t```bash\n 221\tmsw push dev clients/acme/backend
      --force-with-lease\n 222\t```\n 223\t\n 224\tThe force operation is tied to the exact remote
      SHA observed immediately before transfer. A concurrent remote change causes the push to fail
      rather than overwrite it.\n 225\t\n 226\t## What the host-only push transfers\n 227\t\n
      228\t`msw push` transfers only the current committed branch through a verified Git bundle. It
      does not include:\n 229\t\n 230\t- Uncommitted or untracked files\n 231\t- Other local
      branches\n 232\t- Local tags\n 233\t- Your normal Mac Git configuration or hooks\n 234\t- The
      host write token in the VM\n 235\t\n 236\tGit LFS objects referenced by the outgoing commits
      are copied one by one, checked as regular files, verified against their SHA-256 object IDs,
      and uploaded through the host-only credential path.\n 237\t\n 238\tThe privileged host Git
      process runs with an isolated temporary home and no system/global Git configuration, no custom
      hooks, no SSH agent, and no ambient GitHub token environment variable.\n 239\t\n 240\t## Where
      credentials live\n 241\t\n 242\t```text\n 243\tGuest read token: macOS Keychain + MicroSandbox
      host-side secret binding\n 244\tGuest filesystem: placeholder only\n 245\tHost write token:
      macOS Keychain only\n 246\tBackups: neither Keychain token is included\n 247\t```\n 248\t\n
      249\tEvery agent in a VM can use that VM's read capability, but cannot retrieve the actual
      read token through the designed interface and has no host write token.\n 250\t\n 251\t##
      Limits of this model\n 252\t\n 253\t- It prevents guest-authenticated pushes to repositories
      selected in GitHub.\n 254\t- It limits host pushes to repositories selected in the host write
      token.\n 255\t- It does not stop an internet-enabled agent from uploading readable source
      files to an unrelated service.\n 256\t- It does not protect one repository from another
      process inside the same workspace.\n 257\t- GitHub repository rules or organization policies
      can still reject an otherwise authorized host push.\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"FILE
      config.sh\n 1\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n 2\t# MicroSandbox Workspaces configuration.\n 3\t# The
      installer copies this file to ~/.config/msw/config.sh and preserves local edits.\n 4\t\n
      5\tMSW_VERSION=\"3.1.0\"\n 6\t\n 7\t# Shared base image and immutable development snapshot.\n
      8\tMSW_BASE_IMAGE=\"ghcr.io/superradcompany/ubuntu-systemd:24.04\"\n
      9\tMSW_BASE_BUILDER=\"msw-base-builder\"\n 10\tMSW_BASE_SNAPSHOT=\"msw-base-v1\"\n
      11\tMSW_ROOT_DISK=\"48G\"\n 12\t\n 13\t# Friendly browser names. Each VM has a distinct
      loopback IP, so identical ports can\n 14\t# be used concurrently by all workspaces while
      remaining local to this Mac.\n 15\tMSW_DEV_IP=\"127.0.0.10\"\n
      16\tMSW_DEV_HOST=\"dev.msw.test\"\n 17\tMSW_PLAYGROUNDS_IP=\"127.0.0.11\"\n
      18\tMSW_PLAYGROUNDS_HOST=\"playgrounds.msw.test\"\n 19\tMSW_PERSONAL_IP=\"127.0.0.12\"\n
      20\tMSW_PERSONAL_HOST=\"personal.msw.test\"\n 21\t\n 22\t# Workspace CPU defaults plus each
      workspace's live memory limit and resize ceiling.\n 23\tMSW_DEV_CPUS=\"8\"\n
      24\tMSW_DEV_MAX_CPUS=\"12\"\n 25\tMSW_DEV_MEMORY=\"32G\"\n 26\tMSW_DEV_MAX_MEMORY=\"48G\"\n
      27\t\n 28\tMSW_PLAYGROUNDS_CPUS=\"4\"\n 29\tMSW_PLAYGROUNDS_MAX_CPUS=\"12\"\n
      30\tMSW_PLAYGROUNDS_MEMORY=\"32G\"\n 31\tMSW_PLAYGROUNDS_MAX_MEMORY=\"48G\"\n 32\t\n
      33\tMSW_PERSONAL_CPUS=\"6\"\n 34\tMSW_PERSONAL_MAX_CPUS=\"12\"\n
      35\tMSW_PERSONAL_MEMORY=\"16G\"\n 36\tMSW_PERSONAL_MAX_MEMORY=\"32G\"\n 37\t\n 38\t#
      Independent persistent ext4 volumes. Adjust before first installation if needed.\n
      39\tMSW_DEV_WORKSPACE_SIZE=\"120G\"\n 40\tMSW_DEV_RUNTIME_SIZE=\"100G\"\n
      41\tMSW_PLAYGROUNDS_WORKSPACE_SIZE=\"60G\"\n 42\tMSW_PLAYGROUNDS_RUNTIME_SIZE=\"60G\"\n
      43\tMSW_PERSONAL_WORKSPACE_SIZE=\"100G\"\n 44\tMSW_PERSONAL_RUNTIME_SIZE=\"80G\"\n 45\t\n
      46\t# Published on every workspace's own loopback IP.\n 47\t# 24678 and 24679 are reserved for
      automated end-to-end health tests.\n
      48\tMSW_PUBLISHED_PORTS=\"1234,1337,24678-24679,3000-3010,3100,3333,3306-3308,4000-4005,4173,4
      200,4321,5001-5005,5173-5180,5432-5435,5555,6006,6379-6382,7001-7005,8000-8010,8080-8090,8787,
      8888,9000-9005,9229-9230,27017-27019\"\n 49\t\n 50\t# The guest sees a placeholder named
      GH_TOKEN. The real read token stays on the host\n 51\t# and is substituted only when traffic
      reaches one of these GitHub endpoints.\n
      52\tMSW_GITHUB_SECRET_HOSTS=\"github.com,api.github.com\"\n 53\t\n
      54\tMSW_BACKUP_DIR=\"$HOME/Backups/microsandbox\"\n
      55\tMSW_DOCS_DIR=\"$HOME/.local/share/msw/docs\"\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthro
