info  {"category":"agent-session","raw":{"timestamp":"2026-08-07T13:12:38.379Z","type":"event_msg","
      payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"{\"summary\":\"Research memo — complete `msw`
      inventory for a macOS status-bar monitor. Evidence: authoritative dispatch is
      `bin/msw:1809-1888`; command behavior is implemented at `bin/msw:474-1753`; user documentation
      is `docs/MSW-CHEATSHEET.md:3-218`, `docs/SETUP-GUIDE.md:72-323`, and
      `docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md:84-247`. Fixed workspaces are `dev`, `playgrounds`, and `personal`
      (`bin/msw:12,43-59`); paths are restricted beneath `/workspace` (`bin/msw:399-419`).
      Classification below uses RO=pure read-only, RO*=logically read-only but starts a stopped VM,
      M=mutating, D=destructive, and E=temporary mutation restored afterward.\\n\\nGlobal
      dependencies/output: every invocation first requires and sources `MSW_CONFIG_FILE`, defaulting
      to `~/.config/msw/config.sh` (`bin/msw:4-10`). Only help, version, and docs bypass the
      subsequent `msb` executable check (`bin/msw:1813-1817`); even `url`, `ports`, and `github
      status` therefore require installed `msb` when invoked through the wrapper. Configuration
      supplies names/IPs, resource defaults and ceilings, published ports, GitHub secret hosts,
      backup directory, and docs directory (`config.sh:5-55`). The installed CLI was verified as
      `msb 0.6.8`. The wrapper has no global JSON mode and emits human text/ANSI, so the monitor
      should use argument arrays, exit status, and direct read-only `msb` JSON telemetry while
      retaining `msw` for lifecycle mutations because `msw start/restart` injects the host-held
      guest token safely (`bin/msw:331-378,545-560`).\\n\\nInventory:\\n• `msw
      dev|playgrounds|personal [PATH]` and generic `msw shell WORKSPACE [PATH]`: M (may start VM;
      interactive shell can mutate anything), expose only as “Open Terminal” quick actions. PATH
      defaults to `/workspace`; otherwise it must be relative. Output is an interactive SSH/zsh
      session, not parseable. Dependencies: msb, configured sandbox, SSH alias/proxy/key; evidence
      `bin/msw:474-482,1819-1825` and cheatsheet `:5-12`.\\n• `msw zed WORKSPACE [PATH]`: M
      (auto-start plus external-app launch), expose as a project quick action. Default `/workspace`;
      output is only process status. Dependencies: msb, SSH integration, installed executable Zed
      CLI; `bin/msw:484-492,1827-1829`.\\n• `msw exec WORKSPACE COMMAND...`: M/arbitrary and unsafe
      as a generic UI facility; do not expose except internally with a fixed allowlist. It
      auto-starts and runs from `/workspace`; stdout/stderr and exit code belong to the supplied
      command, so there is no stable schema. Dependencies: msb and guest command;
      `bin/msw:381-396,582-587,1869`.\\n• `msw clone WORKSPACE OWNER/REPO [PATH]`: M; omit from the
      primary monitor or place in an advanced repository workflow with validated fields and explicit
      destination review. PATH defaults to repository name; creates parents and refuses an existing
      destination. Git progress is human output; rely on exit status then refresh `repos`.
      Dependencies: running/auto-started VM, guest Git, network, and guest read credential for
      private repositories; `bin/msw:744-747,785-810,1844-1847`.\\n• `msw repos WORKSPACE`: RO*;
      reasonable on-demand project submenu, never background-poll because it starts the VM. Output
      is a sorted newline-delimited list of workspace-relative repository paths; usable cautiously
      as line records but not formal JSON. Dependencies: msb, VM, guest `find/sed/sort`;
      `bin/msw:812-823,1848`.\\n• `msw pull WORKSPACE [PATH|all]`: M; do not expose as a casual menu
      click. Default is `all`; each repository runs `git pull --ff-only --prune`, continuing across
      all and failing overall if any cannot fast-forward. Output is human Git text. Dependencies:
      auto-started VM, guest Git/network/read token; `bin/msw:825-841,1849-1852`.\\n• `msw push
      WORKSPACE PATH [--yes|-y] [--force-with-lease]`: M and remote-destructive when rewriting
      history; normally keep outside the status app. If exposed, require a full review sheet showing
      repository, path, branch, exact commit, fast-forward mode, and commits, preserve typed `PUSH`,
      never pass `--yes`, and add a second warning for force-with-lease. Default is
      fast-forward-only; force uses an exact observed remote SHA. Human summary only, followed by
      success text; no machine schema. Dependencies: auto-started VM, guest and host Git, macOS
      Keychain write token, askpass helper, msb copy, network, and optionally git-lfs/shasum;
      `bin/msw:984-1099` and security boundary `docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md:192-238`.\\n• `msw identity
      \\\"NAME\\\" EMAIL [WORKSPACE|all]`: M; settings-only, not monitor UI. Target defaults to all
      and writes guest global Git configuration. Output is one fixed-width human row per workspace.
      Dependencies: auto-started VM and guest Git; `bin/msw:775-783,1858-1861`.\\n• `msw github`
      with no subcommand and `msw github status [WORKSPACE|all]`: RO; expose optionally as
      credential-health badges. Target defaults to all. Output is a fixed-width table containing
      only guest-read presence, host-write presence, and verification repository—never token values;
      parse conservatively or add a future JSON adapter. Dependencies: msb gate, macOS
      Keychain/security, metadata files; `bin/msw:1311-1320,1343-1356` and
      `docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md:128-134`.\\n• `msw github setup WORKSPACE OWNER/REPO [--read-only]`: M
      and security-sensitive; do not expose in the menu bar, or use a dedicated settings wizard with
      hidden inputs and no captured logs. Default mode requires separate guest-read and host-write
      tokens; `--read-only` removes host write access. It writes Keychain/metadata, changes the VM
      secret binding, quarantines during the transaction, and runs verification with rollback on
      failure. Human interactive output only. Dependencies: msb, Keychain/security, lockf,
      Git/GitHub/network, askpass, existing sandbox; `bin/msw:1194-1309,1346-1349` and
      `docs/GITHUB-SETUP.md:84-126`.\\n• `msw github verify WORKSPACE [OWNER/REPO]`: E/M remotely;
      settings-only with a strong warning because host-write mode creates and deletes a temporary
      remote branch. Repository and access mode default from metadata. It restarts/rebinds the guest
      secret, clones a temporary checkout, verifies guest push rejection, optionally verifies host
      push, and cleans up. Human pass/fail output and exit status only. Same GitHub dependencies as
      setup; `bin/msw:1114-1192,1350-1353`.\\n• `msw github remove WORKSPACE`: D for credentials and
      access; settings-only with explicit confirmation naming the workspace. It deletes metadata and
      both Keychain items, removes the guest secret with restart, and quarantines on incomplete
      cleanup. Human success/error output only. Dependencies: msb, Keychain/security, lockf,
      existing sandbox where present; `bin/msw:1322-1341,1357-1360`.\\n• `msw url WORKSPACE [PORT]
      [http|https]`: RO and ideal as an internal URL builder/copy action. Defaults are port 3000 and
      HTTP; port must be 1–65535 and prepublished. Output is exactly one URL line. Dependencies:
      config plus the wrapper’s msb gate; `bin/msw:467-500,1835-1838`.\\n• `msw open WORKSPACE
      [PORT] [http|https]`: M (auto-start) and core quick action. Same defaults/validation as `url`;
      display the URL before opening and disable unavailable ports. Human note plus macOS browser
      launch, so use exit status rather than parsing. Dependencies: msb, sandbox, `/usr/bin/open`,
      configured hostname; `bin/msw:503-509,1831-1834`.\\n• `msw ports [WORKSPACE|all]`: RO; expose
      as informational/settings data. Target defaults to all. Output is a descriptive human block,
      not machine-readable; the app should read the installed config or derive ports from direct
      `msb status` JSON instead. Published ports and reserved health-check ports are documented at
      `config.sh:46-48` and cheatsheet `:107-117`; implementation
      `bin/msw:447-465,511-526,1839`.\\n• `msw tunnel WORKSPACE REMOTE_PORT [LOCAL_PORT]`: M
      (auto-start plus persistent SSH process); advanced action only, with an active-tunnel row and
      explicit Stop control. LOCAL defaults to REMOTE; both must be 1–65535. Output is a human
      mapping and the process remains attached until cancellation. Dependencies: msb, SSH
      integration, free local port; `bin/msw:625-633,1840-1843`.\\n• `msw start [WORKSPACE|all]`: M
      and core. Target defaults to all; prefer explicit per-workspace actions, confirm “all,”
      serialize operations, and show progress. Human notes plus `<workspace>: running`; refresh
      state from JSON afterward. Dependencies: msb, sandbox and Keychain read token when configured;
      `bin/msw:333-378,528-531,1863`.\\n• `msw stop [WORKSPACE|all]`: M with workload/data-loss risk
      after graceful timeout; core but confirm per workspace and strongly confirm all. Target
      defaults to all; wrapper requests a 60-second graceful stop. Output is a stopping note or
      `<workspace>: already stopped`. Dependencies: msb; `bin/msw:533-543,1864`. Installed `msb
      stop` help confirms timeout expiry can force-kill, risking un-fsynced writes.\\n• `msw restart
      [WORKSPACE|all]`: M with interruption risk; core/advanced with confirmation and progress.
      Target defaults to all and uses a 60-second timeout while preserving guest-token injection.
      Human note only. Dependencies: msb, sandbox, Keychain read token when configured;
